  "image_name": "2ee5e7b7b902127f4ff782405c523960.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how age-related macular degeneration develops in the retina, progressing from a normal state to two advanced forms. It begins with a healthy retinal cross-section, showing the layered organization of ganglion, bipolar, horizontal, and photoreceptor cells above the retinal pigment epithelium, Bruch membrane, and choroidal blood vessels. A small eye diagram indicates that this damage occurs in the macula.\n\nIn early or intermediate dry AMD, deposits called drusen accumulate beneath the retinal pigment epithelium. Immune cells, including microglia within the retina and macrophages below it, appear more prominent, suggesting inflammation around these deposits. From this stage, the disease can move toward either geographic atrophy or neovascular AMD.\n\nGeographic atrophy is depicted as thinning and loss of retinal cells, especially photoreceptors and supporting pigment epithelium, leaving a reduced, disrupted retinal structure. Neovascular AMD instead shows abnormal new blood vessels growing up from the choroid through the barrier below the retina, with associated inflammatory cells. The arrows emphasize that early dry AMD can lead to either late-stage outcome, and that the two advanced forms seem to be related but distinct patterns of retinal degeneration and injury.",
